==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|absent|ee}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌæb.sn̩ˈti/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|iː|s=3}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-absentee.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A person who is [[absent]] from his or her [[employment]], [[school]], [[post]], [[duty]], etc. {{defdate|First attested around 1350 to 1470.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=8}}</ref>
#: {{ux|en|At roll-call there were three '''absentees'''.}}
## {{lb|en|attributive}} {{n-g|Designating a person absent in a particular capacity, sometimes implying that they are difficult to contact, unresponsive, avoiding their responsibilities, etc.}} {{defdate|First attested in the mid 19<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
##: {{co|en|'''absentee''' father; '''absentee''' landlord; '''absentee''' freeholder}}
# Something that is not present where it might be expected.
#: {{ux|en|The manufacturer's promised new model was a notable '''absentee''' at the car show.}}
# {{lb|en|attributive}} {{n-g|Designating something whose owner, person responsible, etc. is absent.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''absentee''' ballot, '''absentee''' property}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|British|historical}} A [[landholder]] who lives in another [[district]] or [[country]] than the one in which his [[estate]] is situated. {{defdate|First attested in the early 17<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1840|author=[[w:George Byron, 6th Baron Byron|Lord Byron]]|chapter=Letter 374: to Mr. Moore (24 May 1820)|title=The Works of Lord Byron: With His Letters and Journals, and His Life|editor=John Murray|page=317|passage=My trustees are going to lend Earl Blessington sixty thousand pounds (at six per cent.) on a Dublin mortgage. Only think of my becoming an Irish '''absentee'''!}}
# A voter who is not present at the time of voting; [[absentee voter]]. {{defdate|First attested in the early 20<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>

